# Engineering standards (every prompt)

Act as senior frontend + backend + DBA + tester + technical writer combined. No shortcuts.

## Delivery method (agile / divide-and-conquer)

1. One vertical slice or module at a time.
2. Finish that slice to **working + tested** before the next.
3. After each new slice: re-test previous slices that touch it (regression).
4. Never stack 5 half-finished features.
5. When human review is needed (security, UX copy, pricing, production deploy, destructive DB): **stop and ask the user** with a short checklist.

## Code quality

- Clean, readable, understandable. Prefer clarity over cleverness.
- **Separation of concerns** — UI ≠ API ≠ DB ≠ domain services.
- Modular: small files, single responsibility. **No 1000+ line dump files** (soft cap ~400 lines; split earlier if mixed concerns).
- Naming reveals purpose (`punches.py`, `LiveBoard.tsx` — not `misc2.js`).
- Comments: why/non-obvious invariants only; keep English; no noise.
- Design patterns only when they earn their keep (Repository/Service for API; not AbstractFactory spam).
- Double-check: no broken imports, dead code, TODO left as “done”.

## Performance & correctness

- Think time/space complexity for hot paths (live board, activity ingest, screenshot upload).
- Avoid N+1 queries; paginate lists; index foreign keys / time columns.
- Screenshots: resize/compress before store; never dump raw 4K into DB.
- Agent: hooks off UI thread; don’t freeze CAD.
- Measure mentally: O(n) scans on every activity tick are wrong — use buckets/aggregates.

## Security

- Auth on every protected route; device token ≠ manager JWT.
- Parameterized queries / ORM only; escape output; validate uploads (type/size).
- No secrets in git; no keystroke text capture; no stealth after Sign Out.
- Server time wins for punches.

## Testing gate (mandatory before “done”)

For each module:

- [ ] Happy path
- [ ] Empty / error / offline (agent)
- [ ] Authz (employee can’t hit manager APIs)
- [ ] Regression on linked modules
- [ ] Brief note in chat: what was tested

## Docs

- Update relevant `docs/*.md` when behavior changes.
- Runbook stays accurate (`docs/22-phase1-runbook.md`).
- New public API → mention in runbook or `docs/10-data-model.md`.

## Folder shape (keep)

```
apps/api/     backend API + domain services
apps/agent/   Windows desktop agent
apps/web/     manager frontend
docs/         product + engineering docs
```

Do not merge frontend into API folders. Do not put business logic only in React components — call APIs/services.

## Tone with user

Guide step-by-step like a mentor: “ab ye test karo → expect X → phir next.” No sugarcoat on broken status.
